{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Three cities you shall set apart for yourself in the midst of your land.</b> And together with those three that Moses set apart in trans-Jordan, they are six cities of refuge. And they must be in an inhabited and prepared place, as it says, ״תכין לך הדרך״ — you shall prepare the way — and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'refuge, refuge' was written at crossroads, so that the unintentional killer may turn there and be saved; and concerning this the verse says (Psalms 25:8): ״טוב וישר ה׳ על כן יורה חטאים בדרך״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, shows sinners the way of salvation, since they did only unintentionally; and similarly it is written further (Job 36:22): ״הן אל ישגיב בכחו מי כמוהו מורה״ — He teaches the way to sinners." "<b>And the sense of</b> ״ושלשת את גבול ארצך״ is that the three lands should be equal in measure, so that the distance from the first to the second should be as much as from the second to the third." "<b>And you must</b> understand here the matter of the six cities of refuge for the unintentional killer: for they correspond to the six directions (קצוות). For the three that Moses set apart have no perfection or consummation in the commandment except together with these three that He now commands to make in the land of Canaan. And the consummation of the commandment is that the cities be six, corresponding to the six directions, alluding to the kindnesses of HaShem in this world — that the cities provide refuge for the body — and likewise for the soul in the World to Come, that it have a portion in the six supernal directions. And even though the commandment is not complete except with six, Moses set apart three corresponding to the three letters of the Name, which turn into six, in the manner of what is written in Sefer Yetzirah: 'three stones build six houses.' For these six cities are a refuge for the body, and in them is an allusion to the six houses that are a refuge for the soul. And further, Moses set apart three cities corresponding to the places from which the soul is hewn, and therefore Moses made these three boundaries to atone for the soul that comes from three supernal places from which it was hewn and separated — for you should know that the soul ascends from Garden to Garden, and from Garden to Eden, and from Eden to Binah; all a trodden path and a straight band; and this is what is written (Ecclesiastes 12:7): ״והרוח תשוב אל האלהים אשר נתנה״ — that is Binah, where is the end of its rest. And this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 73:24): ״בעצתך תנחני״ — in this world — ״ואחר כבוד תקחני״, that is, and after You will take me to glory — that is the supernal Garden — You will take me. And 'with Elohim he speaks' (עם האלהים ידבר), which he mentioned at the head of the psalm: ״אך טוב לישראל אלהים״, and concerning this it says here ״תכין לך הדרך״ — prepare for yourself in this world the way to the World to Come."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "The phrase ועם האלהים ידבר ('and with Elohim he speaks') appears to cite or echo a phrase associated with the psalm; its exact syntactic role in the sentence is uncertain but rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:28.026721783Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}